      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "Elections and terms",
      "body": "Raft cuts time into numbered terms, and each term starts with a leader election [1]. A follower that hears no heartbeat for its election timeout increases the term counter, votes for itself and asks every other server for its vote [2]. Each server votes once per term [3], a candidate with a majority becomes leader [4], and because the timeout is randomized, split votes are resolved quickly [5]. etcd defaults to a 100 ms heartbeat and a 1000 ms election timeout [6][7].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)",
          "title": "Raft (algorithm), Wikipedia",
          "quote": "Each term starts with a leader election."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)",
          "title": "Raft (algorithm), Wikipedia",
          "quote": "It starts the election by increasing the term counter, voting for itself as new leader, and sending a message to all other servers requesting their vote."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)",
          "title": "Raft (algorithm), Wikipedia",
          "quote": "A server will vote only once per term, on a first-come-first-served basis."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/consul/docs/architecture/consensus",
          "title": "Consensus, Consul documentation (HashiCorp)",
          "quote": "If a candidate receives a quorum of votes, then it is promoted to leader."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)",
          "title": "Raft (algorithm), Wikipedia",
          "quote": "Raft uses a randomized election timeout to ensure that split vote problems are resolved quickly."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://etcd.io/docs/v3.5/tuning/",
          "title": "Tuning, etcd v3.5 documentation",
          "quote": "By default, etcd uses a 100ms heartbeat interval."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://etcd.io/docs/v3.5/tuning/",
          "title": "Tuning, etcd v3.5 documentation",
          "quote": "By default, etcd uses a 1000ms election timeout."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "heading": "Commit on a majority",
      "body": "Every write goes to the leader, which appends it to its log and replicates it to the followers [1]. An entry is committed once it is durably stored on a majority, and only then is it applied [2]. So 5 servers keep working with 2 down; with 3 down they stop making progress, but never return a wrong result [3][4]. After a leader crash, the new leader repairs any mismatch by forcing the followers to duplicate its own log [5].",
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/consul/docs/architecture/consensus",
          "title": "Consensus, Consul documentation (HashiCorp)",
          "quote": "A client can request that a leader append a new log entry."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://developer.hashicorp.com/consul/docs/architecture/consensus",
          "title": "Consensus, Consul documentation (HashiCorp)",
          "quote": "An entry is considered committed when it is durably stored on a quorum of nodes."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://raft.github.io/",
          "title": "The Raft Consensus Algorithm (raft.github.io)",
          "quote": "a cluster of 5 servers can continue to operate even if 2 servers fail"
        },
        {
          "url": "https://raft.github.io/",
          "title": "The Raft Consensus Algorithm (raft.github.io)",
          "quote": "If more servers fail, they stop making progress (but will never return an incorrect result)."
        },
        {
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)",
          "title": "Raft (algorithm), Wikipedia",
          "quote": "The new leader will then handle inconsistency by forcing the followers to duplicate its own log."
        }
      ]
